  <span class="chapter" data-osis-id="PHM 1" data-n="1"></span>
  <div class="title s1" data-title-type="s1" data-style="s1">Greetings from Paul and Timothy</div>
  <p class="p" data-style="p">
    <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:1" data-n="1"></span>Paul, a prisoner of Christ Jesus, and Timothy our brother,</p>
  <p class="p" data-style="p">To Philemon our beloved fellow worker, <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:2" data-n="2"></span>to Apphia our sister, to Archippus our fellow soldier, and to the church that meets at your<span class="note footnote" data-note-type="footnote" data-n="+" data-style="f"><span class="char" data-style="fr">1:2 </span><span class="char" data-style="fqa">You </span><span class="char" data-style="ft">and </span><span class="char" data-style="fqa">Your </span><span class="char" data-style="ft">are singular throughout this letter, except in verses 3, 22, and 25.</span></span> house:</p>
  <p class="p" data-style="p">
    <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:3" data-n="3"></span>Grace and peace to you from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ.</p>
  <div class="title s1" data-title-type="s1" data-style="s1">Philemon’s Faith and Love</div>
  <p class="p" data-style="p">
    <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:4" data-n="4"></span>I always thank my God, remembering you in my prayers, <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:5" data-n="5"></span>because I hear about your faith in the Lord Jesus and your love for all the saints. <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:6" data-n="6"></span>I pray that your partnership in the faith may become effective as you fully acknowledge every good thing that is ours in Christ. <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:7" data-n="7"></span>I take great joy and encouragement in your love, because you, brother, have refreshed the hearts of the saints.</p>
  <div class="title s1" data-title-type="s1" data-style="s1">Paul’s Appeal for Onesimus</div>
  <p class="p" data-style="p">
    <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:8" data-n="8"></span>So although in Christ I am bold enough to order you to do what is proper, <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:9" data-n="9"></span>I prefer to appeal on the basis of love. For I, Paul, am now aged, and a prisoner of Christ Jesus as well.</p>
  <p class="p" data-style="p">
    <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:10" data-n="10"></span>I appeal to you for my child Onesimus,<span class="note footnote" data-note-type="footnote" data-n="+" data-style="f"><span class="char" data-style="fr">1:10 </span><span class="char" data-style="fqa">Onesimus </span><span class="char" data-style="ft">means </span><span class="char" data-style="fqa">useful </span><span class="char" data-style="ft">(see verse 11) or </span><span class="char" data-style="fqa">beneficial </span><span class="char" data-style="ft">(see verse 20).</span></span> whose father I became while I was in chains. <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:11" data-n="11"></span>Formerly he was useless to you, but now he has become useful both to you and to me. <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:12" data-n="12"></span>I am sending back to you him who is my very heart.</p>
  <p class="p" data-style="p">
    <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:13" data-n="13"></span>I would have liked to keep him with me, so that on your behalf he could minister to me in my chains for the gospel. <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:14" data-n="14"></span>But I did not want to do anything without your consent, so that your goodness will not be out of compulsion, but by your own free will. <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:15" data-n="15"></span>For perhaps this is why he was separated from you for a while, so that you might have him back for good — <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:16" data-n="16"></span>no longer as a slave, but better than a slave, as a beloved brother. He is especially beloved to me, but even more so to you, both in person and in the Lord.</p>
  <p class="p" data-style="p">
    <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:17" data-n="17"></span>So if you consider me a partner, receive him as you would receive me. <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:18" data-n="18"></span>But if he has wronged you in any way or owes you anything, charge it to my account. <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:19" data-n="19"></span>I, Paul, write this with my own hand. I will repay it— not to mention that you owe me your very self.</p>
  <p class="p" data-style="p">
    <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:20" data-n="20"></span>Yes, brother, let me have some benefit<span class="note footnote" data-note-type="footnote" data-n="+" data-style="f"><span class="char" data-style="fr">1:20 </span><span class="char" data-style="ft">Greek </span><span class="char" data-style="fqa">onaimēn</span><span class="char" data-style="ft">, a play on the name </span><span class="char" data-style="fqa">Onesimus</span></span> from you in the Lord. Refresh my heart in Christ.</p>
  <p class="p" data-style="p">
    <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:21" data-n="21"></span>Confident of your obedience, I write to you, knowing that you will do even more than I ask.</p>
  <p class="p" data-style="p">
    <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:22" data-n="22"></span>In the meantime, prepare a guest room for me, because I hope that through your prayers I will be restored to you.</p>
  <div class="title s1" data-title-type="s1" data-style="s1">Additional Greetings</div>
  <p class="p" data-style="p">
    <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:23" data-n="23"></span>Epaphras, my fellow prisoner in Christ Jesus, sends you greetings, <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:24" data-n="24"></span>as do Mark, Aristarchus, Demas, and Luke, my fellow workers.</p>
  <p class="p" data-style="p">
    <span class="verse" data-osis-id="PHM 1:25" data-n="25"></span>The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ be with your spirit.<span class="note footnote" data-note-type="footnote" data-n="+" data-style="f"><span class="char" data-style="fr">1:25 </span><span class="char" data-style="ft">BYZ and TR include </span><span class="char" data-style="fqa">Amen.</span></span></p>
  

#### verses ####
1:1-1 marker=/1/21/1 start=/1/18 end=/1/23/1 "Greetings from Paul and Timothy\nPaul, a prisoner of Christ Jesus, and Timothy our brother,\nTo Philemon our beloved fellow worker,"
1:2-2 marker=/1/23/2 start=/1/23/2 end=/1/23/6 "to Apphia our sister, to Archippus our fellow soldier, and to the church that meets at your [1:2 You and Your are singular throughout this letter, except in verses 3, 22, and 25.] house:"
1:3-3 marker=/1/25/1 start=/1/23/7 end=/1/25/3 "Grace and peace to you from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ."
1:4-4 marker=/1/29/1 start=/1/25/4 end=/1/29/3 "Philemon’s Faith and Love\nI always thank my God, remembering you in my prayers,"
1:5-5 marker=/1/29/4 start=/1/29/4 end=/1/29/6 "because I hear about your faith in the Lord Jesus and your love for all the saints."
1:6-6 marker=/1/29/7 start=/1/29/7 end=/1/29/9 "I pray that your partnership in the faith may become effective as you fully acknowledge every good thing that is ours in Christ."
1:7-7 marker=/1/29/10 start=/1/29/10 end=/1/29/12 "I take great joy and encouragement in your love, because you, brother, have refreshed the hearts of the saints."
1:8-8 marker=/1/33/1 start=/1/29/13 end=/1/33/3 "Paul’s Appeal for Onesimus\nSo although in Christ I am bold enough to order you to do what is proper,"
1:9-9 marker=/1/33/4 start=/1/33/4 end=/1/33/6 "I prefer to appeal on the basis of love. For I, Paul, am now aged, and a prisoner of Christ Jesus as well."
1:10-10 marker=/1/35/1 start=/1/33/7 end=/1/35/5 "I appeal to you for my child Onesimus, [1:10 Onesimus means useful (see verse 11) or beneficial (see verse 20).] whose father I became while I was in chains."
1:11-11 marker=/1/35/6 start=/1/35/6 end=/1/35/8 "Formerly he was useless to you, but now he has become useful both to you and to me."
1:12-12 marker=/1/35/9 start=/1/35/9 end=/1/35/11 "I am sending back to you him who is my very heart."
1:13-13 marker=/1/37/1 start=/1/35/12 end=/1/37/3 "I would have liked to keep him with me, so that on your behalf he could minister to me in my chains for the gospel."
1:14-14 marker=/1/37/4 start=/1/37/4 end=/1/37/6 "But I did not want to do anything without your consent, so that your goodness will not be out of compulsion, but by your own free will."
1:15-15 marker=/1/37/7 start=/1/37/7 end=/1/37/9 "For perhaps this is why he was separated from you for a while, so that you might have him back for good —"
1:16-16 marker=/1/37/10 start=/1/37/10 end=/1/37/12 "no longer as a slave, but better than a slave, as a beloved brother. He is especially beloved to me, but even more so to you, both in person and in the Lord."
1:17-17 marker=/1/39/1 start=/1/37/13 end=/1/39/3 "So if you consider me a partner, receive him as you would receive me."
1:18-18 marker=/1/39/4 start=/1/39/4 end=/1/39/6 "But if he has wronged you in any way or owes you anything, charge it to my account."
1:19-19 marker=/1/39/7 start=/1/39/7 end=/1/39/9 "I, Paul, write this with my own hand. I will repay it— not to mention that you owe me your very self."
1:20-20 marker=/1/41/1 start=/1/39/10 end=/1/41/5 "Yes, brother, let me have some benefit [1:20 Greek onaimēn, a play on the name Onesimus] from you in the Lord. Refresh my heart in Christ."
1:21-21 marker=/1/43/1 start=/1/41/6 end=/1/43/3 "Confident of your obedience, I write to you, knowing that you will do even more than I ask."
1:22-22 marker=/1/45/1 start=/1/43/4 end=/1/45/3 "In the meantime, prepare a guest room for me, because I hope that through your prayers I will be restored to you."
1:23-23 marker=/1/49/1 start=/1/45/4 end=/1/49/3 "Additional Greetings\nEpaphras, my fellow prisoner in Christ Jesus, sends you greetings,"
1:24-24 marker=/1/49/4 start=/1/49/4 end=/1/49/6 "as do Mark, Aristarchus, Demas, and Luke, my fellow workers."
1:25-25 marker=/1/51/1 start=/1/49/7 end=/1/51/4 "The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ be with your spirit. [1:25 BYZ and TR include Amen.]"
